Cortisol patterns among children were divided into three groups: hypersecretion (68%), characterized by a sustained elevation of cortisol; hypo-secretion (9%), characterized by a sustained decrease in cortisol; and hypersecretion-to-hyporecretion (23%), where cortisol levels start high and then return to normal. Children in the hypersecretion-to-hyporecretion group experienced fewer psychological health issues (both internal and external symptoms) compared to those in the hypersecretion group. Children in the hypo-secretion group showed no significant differences in psychological health symptoms compared to those in the continuously high cortisol group.
